Source package: ptyxis

search-next-on-unshifted-key.patch
    The unshifted search key moves to the next match and the shifted one to the previous.
    Upstream names its actions after screen direction rather than search order, so search.up runs
    ptyxis_find_bar_previous, and it binds Ctrl+G and Return to search.up.

copy-as-html-shortcut.patch
    Makes Copy as HTML reachable from the keyboard. The clipboard.copy-as-html action is offered
    in the context menu but has no entry in org.gnome.Ptyxis.Shortcuts, so only the mouse reaches
    it. The patch adds the schema key and leaves it empty, so the binding is chosen by
    configure-ptyxis.sh.

palette-highlight-colours.patch
    Lets a palette set the selection highlight colours, which Ptyxis otherwise leaves to the
    toolkit. Both keys are optional and read exactly as the cursor colours are, so palettes that
    omit them are unaffected.

visual-bell-without-animations.patch
    Makes the visual bell visible with animations disabled. The header flash is a CSS animation,
    which GTK does not run while gtk-enable-animations is off, so the bell showed nothing. The
    header then shows the bell colours for 150 ms.

working-directory-from-proc.patch
    Opens a new tab or window in the active tab's directory even when the shell has not reported
    it with OSC 7, for example after a terminal reset. The directory of the foreground process is
    then read from /proc by ptyxis-agent, as Ubuntu's gnome-terminal did. Upstream relies on
    OSC 7 alone and leaves the agent's GetWorkingDirectory unused.

register-app-id-before-gtk-init.patch
    Makes the dialog shown before logging out or shutting down list Ptyxis while a program runs
    in one of its tabs. Ptyxis registers a logout inhibitor for it, but the portal knows Ptyxis
    by an empty application ID, because Ptyxis calls gtk_init before GTK registers the ID with
    the portal, and GNOME Shell leaves out inhibitors whose ID names no application.

restore-session-on-new-window-action.patch
    Upstream's commit b4777fd, on main but in no release yet. GNOME Shell starts Ptyxis from the
    dock and the overview with its new-window action rather than by activating it, and Ptyxis
    restored its session only when activated, so a terminal started that way always opened a
    blank window (Ptyxis issue 560). The action now restores the session first when no window
    is open. restore-window-placement.patch adds that the hidden windows GTK creates for its
    restored window state do not count as open windows. Drop the patch once Ubuntu ships a
    Ptyxis with the commit.

save-session-on-change.patch
    Saves the session, the windows with their tabs and the directory of each tab, 3 seconds after
    it changes. Upstream saves it only when the last window is closed, so after a crash, a reset
    of the machine or the end of the desktop session, at which Ptyxis exits without closing its
    windows, the next start restores the tabs of the last time all windows were closed, which
    can be days old (Ptyxis issue 603). A window created for a single command, such as one
    started from a .desktop file with Terminal=true, is left out of the session, since it would
    be restored as a shell without its command. Nothing is saved this way until the saved
    session is restored, so that a Ptyxis started for something else does not replace it.

restore-window-placement.patch
    Lets Ptyxis's windows return to their workspace, monitor, position and size when it restores
    its session, with the session save/restore API of GtkApplication, which only the GTK of
    patches/gtk4/ has, the same way as patches/gnome-text-editor/restore-window-placement.patch
    does for Text Editor. Every window has an ID, saved as window-id both with the window in
    Ptyxis's session state, ~/.config/org.gnome.Ptyxis/session.gvariant, and in GTK's window
    state of it, ~/.local/state/org.gnome.Ptyxis@fallback.state, which links the two. GTK
    restores the names under which mutter remembers where each window was, and Ptyxis gives
    each restored window the tabs saved with the same ID. GTK saves its window state every 15
    seconds while a Ptyxis window has the keyboard focus, so a window opened less than 15 seconds
    before a crash opens unplaced. The property and the signals of the API are looked up at
    runtime, so the package builds against Ubuntu's GTK and behaves as before with it. A
    standalone instance (--standalone) does not use the API.

    After a crash of the whole system, a window returns to its place only if mutter stored it,
    which it does when the window changes only with
    patches/mutter/save-toplevels-on-change.patch.

restore-screens.patch
    Saves what each tab shows, the text of its last screen without colors, with the session,
    and shows it dimmed above the prompt of the tab's new shell when the session is restored,
    as Terminal on macOS does, so that it is clear what each terminal was doing. The screens
    are kept in ~/.local/state/org.gnome.Ptyxis/screens.gvariant, readable only by you, and
    they are shown only after a session which ended with windows open: closing the last window
    removes them, and the next start restores its tabs without them. A terminal scrolled back
    keeps the screen it had the last time it was scrolled to the end. The dim style is VTE's,
    which scales the foreground to 2/3: on tango-black it turns the text grey, while on a
    light palette it would darken the text instead.

None of this is reachable through settings, which is why the patches exist. A shortcut in Ptyxis
is spread across four places, and only the first is visible from outside:

  the Shortcuts schema        the key, and its default
  ptyxis-shortcuts.defs       a macro turning that key into a GObject property
  a GtkShortcut in a .ui      the binding, with its trigger looked up from that property
  an id on the menu item      what ptyxis_shortcuts_update_menu matches to show the accelerator

Some bindings skip the schema entirely and are registered in code with
gtk_widget_class_add_binding_action, which is the case for Ctrl+G. Those are invisible to
gsettings, to the preferences window, and to the conflict check in configure-ptyxis.sh.

Ptyxis ships no test suite, so there is nothing to add tests to. The three session patches are
tested together with patches/gtk4/ and patches/mutter/ by tests/run-ptyxis-session.sh, see
tests/README. For the others a build failure is the only automated check, and for changes like
these it is a weak one: every patch here could apply, compile, and still behave wrongly. Check by
hand after a rebuild.

  Ctrl+F then Ctrl+G           should move down the scrollback, towards newer output
  select text, Ctrl+Shift+C    should copy as HTML, and the context menu should show that accel
  select text                  should highlight dark red (#a40000) with white text
  printf '\a', animations off  should colour the header for 150 ms
  cd /tmp, then printf '\e]7;\e\\'; sleep 60, and Control+N while sleep runs
                               should open in /tmp, not in the home directory
  sleep 60, then Power Off in the system menu
                               should list Terminal with "A foreground process is running"
  two windows on different workspaces, ls in each, wait 15 seconds, run pkill -KILL -x ptyxis
  in one of them, and start Terminal
                               should bring back both windows on their workspaces, each tab in
                               its directory with the ls output dimmed above a new prompt
  close the last window with its close button, start Terminal
                               should bring back its tabs without the dimmed screens

The first start after installing restore-window-placement.patch has no window names saved yet,
so the windows are placed from the second start on.

After a version bump, check that ptyxis_window_close_request and
update_visible_and_maybe_close in src/ptyxis-window.c are still the only places that save the
session, and that ptyxis_session_restore in src/ptyxis-session.c still creates each window with
ptyxis_window_new_empty before adding its tabs.
